  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2023 preprint is showing early but focused scholarly uptake: it has 6 citations, 7 documented downstream reuse events, and is being cited by recent reviews and research articles on KMT2A-rearranged leukemia, menin inhibition, lineage switching, and targeted-therapy resistance. The citation record is still small, but the pattern points to relevance within active leukemia and epigenetics research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Janssens and colleagues examine how KMT2A oncoproteins can drive epigenetic resistance to targeted therapies in KMT2A-rearranged leukemia. Using profiling across 36 representative samples, including cases with therapy-associated lymphoid-to-myeloid lineage switching, the study addresses a mechanistic problem that sits at the intersection of chromatin regulation, treatment resistance, and disease plasticity. That combination of topic and approach appears to be resonating with researchers working on acute myeloid leukemia and broader KMT2A biology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is not volume but concentration. The work has already been cited in six articles published across 2024 to 2026, in venues such as Cancers, Medical Oncology, Biomedicines, Journal of Clinical Medicine, Blood Science, and Inflammation Research. These citing papers cluster around acute myeloid leukemia research, protein degradation and inhibitor strategies, and lineage-switching or KMT2A-focused therapeutic discussions, suggesting that the preprint is being used as a mechanistic reference point in a lively and medically relevant literature.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse signals reinforce that picture. Beyond citations, the record shows 7 downstream reuse events, including one biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. The work also appears in repository and indexing services, with discovery through PubMed reflected in the record. Together, these signals indicate that the study is circulating within specialist biomedical channels even though public discussion and scite coverage remain limited in the available data.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the article\u2019s influence is best described as early-stage but clearly targeted. It is not yet a high-volume citation item, and the available evidence does not support any percentile or ranking claim. Still, the citations that do exist are structurally meaningful: they come from recent, domain-relevant articles that are building on questions of KMT2A-mediated transcriptional control, therapeutic resistance, and next-generation strategies for acute leukemia.</p>",
